<TopicPage slug="subjects/audiology/foundational/hearing-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Audiology is built on hearing science: what sound physically is, how the ear converts it
    into nerve signals, and where in that chain things fail. Hearing feels effortless — a
    conversation simply arrives — but it is the end product of a physics problem (a pressure
    wave travelling through air), a mechanical problem (channelling that wave from a thin gas
    into salt water without losing it), and an electrochemical problem (converting motion into
    nerve spikes the brain can read). Each stage is specialised, and each is a place where the
    system can break.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does an audiologist need this physics and anatomy? Because every clinical fact is a map
    drawn over it. An audiogram is not an abstract chart: its frequency axis is the cochlea's
    geography, and its thresholds measure which parts of that geography answer the bell. The
    classification of hearing loss — conductive, sensorineural, mixed — is literally a
    diagnosis of <em>which link broke</em>: outer or middle ear (sound never arrives properly),
    cochlea or nerve (sound arrives but is never converted), or both. Presbycusis's high-frequency
    slope, noise damage's 4 kHz notch, otosclerosis's air–bone gap — each is anatomy and physics
    showing up in a measurement.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page walks the chain in the order sound experiences it: the physics of pressure waves,
    the anatomy that captures and transduces them, and the psychoacoustics — the perception
    layer where measurements become experiences like loudness, pitch and the ability to pick a
    voice out of a party. Together they form the vocabulary every later clinical unit assumes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the auditory chain, ear to cortex" widget="equationStep" config={hearingCfg} />

  <h2>Physics of sound</h2>
  <p>
    Sound is organised vibration travelling through a medium — usually air. A vibrating object
    (a guitar string, a vocal fold) pushes and pulls the air around it, creating alternating
    zones of compression and rarefaction that propagate outward as a pressure wave. Two
    quantities describe most of what the ear does with it: <strong>frequency</strong>, the
    number of compression cycles per second in hertz (Hz), heard as pitch; and
    <strong>intensity</strong>, the pressure amplitude of the wave, heard as loudness. Human
    hearing spans roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz — a keyboard's lowest note sits near 27 Hz and its
    top near 4,200 Hz, but harmonics well above speech's fundamentals carry its brightness.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intensity scale is the first numerical habit to build. Sound pressure spans a
    million-fold range between the threshold of hearing and the threshold of pain, so it is
    compressed logarithmically into decibels: dB SPL = 20·log₁₀(p / 20 µPa), where 20
    micropascals (the pressure of a mosquito at three metres, roughly) defines 0 dB SPL.
    Consequences students must internalise: +6 dB is a doubling of <em>pressure</em>, +20 dB is
    a tenfold pressure increase, +10 dB is roughly a perceived doubling of <em>loudness</em>,
    and the difference between a whisper (about 30 dB SPL) and a jet engine at close range
    (about 140 dB SPL) is a factor of ten million in pressure. The curve below plots pressure
    against its decibel value — the log scale is what makes that range plottable at all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="sound pressure converted to the decibel scale" widget="plotter" config={eqCfg} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> 0 dB means silence. Zero on a logarithmic scale means "at
    reference level", not "nothing" — and negative dB SPL is a perfectly meaningful (inaudible)
    pressure. The decibel is a ratio, never an absolute quantity by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Anatomy of the auditory system</h2>
  <p>
    The ear is conventionally taught in three parts, and each part solves a specific problem.
    The <strong>outer ear</strong> — pinna and ear canal — is a collector and a signal
    processor: the pinna's folds funnel sound and, crucially, filter it differently depending
    on elevation and direction, giving the brain cues for locating sound in three dimensions;
    the canal, a tube about 2.5 cm long, resonates near 2.7 kHz and boosts exactly the
    frequency band where speech consonants live — one reason that band matters so much for
    intelligibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>middle ear</strong> solves an impedance problem. Sound in air meets the fluid
    of the inner ear, and without help over 99 per cent of the energy would reflect away — the
    same physics that makes a shout over a swimming pool barely ripple the water. Three tiny
    bones (malleus, incus, stapes — the body's smallest) form a lever system, and the eardrum's
    area concentrating onto the stapes footplate's much smaller area multiplies the pressure;
    together these match the air-to-fluid transition with about 22 dB of gain. Two muscles
    (stapedius and tensor tympani) stiffen the chain reflexively at loud sounds — a dampener,
    though too slow against impulsive noise.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>inner ear</strong> is where mechanics become biology. The snail-shaped cochlea
    contains the basilar membrane, whose stiffness varies along its length: stiff and narrow at
    the base (resonating with high frequencies), wide and floppy at the apex (low frequencies).
    A sound entering the cochlea sets up a travelling wave that peaks at the place matching its
    frequency — the ear is a biological frequency analyser, and this <em>tonotopic</em> map is
    the single most important fact in the chapter. Resting on the membrane sits the organ of
    Corti with its hair cells: one row of <strong>inner hair cells</strong>, which transduce
    motion into nerve spikes (each bending of their stereocilia opens ion channels), and three
    rows of <strong>outer hair cells</strong>, which actively amplify and sharpen the travelling
    wave — the cochlea is not a passive microphone but a powered one. From there the auditory
    nerve and brainstem preserve frequency and timing maps all the way to the cortex.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the ear "hears" the way a microphone records. The cochlea
    actively edits: outer hair cells boost weak signals, compression handles an enormous
    dynamic range, and the brain performs pattern analysis on the result. This is why sensorineural
    loss is not just "quieter" — damage to the active amplifier distorts, compresses and
    smears, which no simple amplification fully restores.
  </p>

  <h2>Psychoacoustics: from physics to perception</h2>
  <p>
    Psychoacoustics measures the mapping between physical quantities and experiences. Three
    mappings carry most clinical weight. <strong>Loudness</strong> is not intensity: the ear's
    sensitivity varies with frequency, captured in equal-loudness contours — at low levels the
    ear is nearly deaf to deep bass (a 40 Hz tone needs far more pressure than a 2 kHz tone to
    sound equally loud), which is why hearing losses of equal dB feel unequal across pitches,
    and why hearing aids are fitted per frequency band.
    <strong>Pitch</strong> follows frequency but reads the tonotopic place code and timing
    together; damage to a cochlear region removes its pitch range, which is why high-frequency
    loss costs consonant clarity before it costs "hearing".
    <strong>Masking</strong> — one sound raising the threshold of another — is the everyday
    experience of not hearing someone talk over the dishwasher; its clinical use is profound,
    because masking noise in the non-test ear is what makes threshold measurement honest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Binaural hearing adds the spatial layer: the brain localises sound using interaural time
    differences (about 0.6 ms maximum, precise for low frequencies) and level differences
    (heads shadow high frequencies), plus the pinna's spectral cues for height. This is why
    two hearing aids beat one for understanding speech in noise, and why unilateral loss is a
    real disability even with a normal other ear — the brain's noise-separation engine needs
    two inputs.
  </p>

  <h2>From sound to speech</h2>
  <p>
    Why does the frequency axis matter so much clinically? Because speech does not spread
    its information evenly across it. Vowels are low-frequency events (their energy sits
    mostly around 250–1,000 Hz) and are loud and easy to hear; consonants — the
    information-bearing workhorses of intelligibility — live largely in the higher bands
    (the /s/ of "six", the /f/ of "five", the /θ/ of "thin" cluster around 4–8 kHz) and are
    quieter. The consequences run through audiology: a patient with a high-frequency loss
    hears that people are talking but cannot tell "fit" from "sit" from "thick", and often
    reports "everyone mumbles" — a perfect description of what a slope in the audiogram
    does to the frequency importance map. It is also why hearing aids are programmed per
    frequency band (amplifying only where the loss lives, leaving healthy regions alone)
    and why the speech banana — the overlay showing where speech's frequencies and levels
    fall on the audiogram — is one of the field's standard counselling tools: draw the
    patient's thresholds over it and the conversation about their hearing becomes a picture
    of which sounds they are missing.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The active cochlea explains the clinic's signature findings.</strong> Outer hair
    cells' amplification is frequency-sharp and level-dependent — strong for quiet sounds,
    backing off for loud ones — which produces the cochlea's enormous ~120 dB dynamic range
    despite hair cells being individually limited. When outer hair cells die (noise, ageing,
    ototoxic drugs), thresholds rise and that graceful compression is lost:
    <em>recruitment</em> — loudness growing abnormally fast once sound exceeds the damaged
    threshold — is the classic perceptual consequence, and it is why aggressive hearing-aid
    compression must be fitted carefully.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The 4 kHz notch is anatomy you can read.</strong> Noise-induced loss characteristically
    dips near 3–6 kHz, deepest around 4 kHz: the canal's resonance focuses energy there, and
    the basal region of the cochlea is most metabolically vulnerable. A notch with recovery at
    8 kHz is so characteristic that it identifies a noise history from the audiogram alone.
    Similarly, otoacoustic emissions — faint sounds the healthy cochlea itself emits, produced
    by the outer hair cells — give clinicians a pre-neural, cochlea-specific test used in
    newborn hearing screening: evidence that the "active ear" model is not just theory but a
    clinical instrument.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Central processing extends the chain past the nerve.</strong> Brainstem responses
    (the ABR) time-lock to stimulus features within milliseconds and let clinicians estimate
    thresholds in patients who cannot respond; cortical processing handles scene analysis —
    separating sources, exploiting attention. A frontier called "hidden hearing loss" proposes
    that some patients with normal audiograms have damaged synapses between hair cells and
    nerve fibres, degrading speech-in-noise performance while sparing pure-tone thresholds —
    a reminder that the audiogram, powerful as it is, samples the chain rather than exhausting
    it. Later units add the vestibular half of the inner ear and full diagnostic audiology, but
    every test on the clinic floor stands on the anatomy and physics this lesson maps.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>One scale footnote that prevents endless confusion: dB SPL versus dB HL.</strong>
    This page defined dB SPL against a fixed physical reference (20 µPa); the clinical
    audiogram, however, is printed in dB <em>HL</em> (hearing level), a per-frequency scale
    whose zero has been shifted so that 0 dB HL is the <em>average normal-hearing
    threshold</em> at each frequency — absorbing the ear's own sensitivity curve so that a
    normal audiogram reads flat across the top. The two scales differ by a frequency-specific
    offset (largest in the low frequencies and around 6 kHz, where human hearing is
    especially sharp or variable). The practical rule: physical acoustics is measured in dB
    SPL, clinical results are reported in dB HL, and every calibration and counselling step
    in audiology involves knowing which scale a number belongs to — a small footnote that is
    really the whole physics-versus-clinic bridge in one table.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASHA/CAA accreditation standards; Pickles, <em>An Introduction to the Physiology of Hearing</em>; Musiek &amp; Baran, <em>The Auditory System</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
